WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.440
Drone operations are the planned and controlled use of an unmanned aircraft system to

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:06.055
accomplish a defined purpose.

00:00:06.055 --> 00:00:09.880
The system includes more than the aircraft: it also includes the controller, software,

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:13.035
communications, batteries, sensors, payloads, records,

00:00:13.035 --> 00:00:15.105
and people responsible for the flight.

00:00:15.105 --> 00:00:18.640
Operator roles may include a pilot in command, visual observer, payload operator,

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:20.540
mission lead, or data reviewer.

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:24.630
Even a small recreational flight concentrates several responsibilities in one person.

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:28.704
Each role needs a shared understanding of the plan, limits, and emergency actions.

00:00:28.704 --> 00:00:33.375
Drones support recreation, inspection, mapping, photography, research, public safety,

00:00:33.375 --> 00:00:35.550
security, and many other legitimate uses.

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:39.580
The same aircraft may be suitable for one mission and inappropriate for another because

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:39.920
weather,

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:43.250
airspace, people, payload, or data sensitivity differ.

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:45.200
Successful operation begins before takeoff.

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:48.473
The operator defines the purpose, verifies the aircraft and crew are ready,

00:00:48.473 --> 00:00:51.900
reviews the location and current official requirements, assesses hazards,

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.235
and selects conservative limits for the mission.

00:00:54.235 --> 00:00:57.363
Safety, legality, and accountability are core principles.

00:00:57.363 --> 00:01:01.915
Technical capability does not create permission to fly anywhere or collect any data.

00:01:01.915 --> 00:01:05.770
Operators must respect aviation rules, property, privacy, people, other aircraft,

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:07.880
and the limits of their equipment and training.

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:11.224
The goal is a useful flight completed without creating unacceptable risk.

00:01:11.224 --> 00:01:14.965
Good operators are willing to delay, change, or cancel a mission when conditions,

00:01:14.965 --> 00:01:18.760
authorization, equipment, or crew readiness do not support safe operation.
